WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:04.910
"Vikings: Valhalla" has finally arrived, and
it's packed with parallels to its predecessor.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:06.640
What's up with Jarl's rune?

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.250
Why is that chain so familiar?

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.110
Answers, straight ahead.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:13.629
Kattegat was just a small port town when we
first saw it in "Vikings," but the former

00:00:13.629 --> 00:00:18.620
seat of Ragnar Lothbrok has grown into a bustling,
multicultural center of trade by the time

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.420
"Vikings: Valhalla" begins, spreading from
the shore of the fjord right up into the valley

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.420
beyond.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:27.180
"It is more beautiful than I ever imagined."

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:31.020
It's a free city and the center of the Viking
world in Norway, and as such, it needs to

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.029
be protected.

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:36.320
The location comes with natural defenses — notably,
the narrow entryway makes it difficult for

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.379
a fleet of invading ships to enter.

00:00:38.379 --> 00:00:41.649
It's better to be safe than sorry, however,
which is why there's a new addition to the

00:00:41.649 --> 00:00:45.170
port that was apparently inspired by Ragnar's
greatest defeat.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:48.819
Ragnar's raid of Paris was one of the standout
moments on "Vikings" — he fakes his death

00:00:48.819 --> 00:00:53.969
and enters the city in a coffin, having undergone
a sham conversion prior to his staged passing.

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:57.609
But when he returns to the Frankish capital
a second time, he is outsmarted by a thick

00:00:57.609 --> 00:01:01.730
chain the Franks had installed across the
river Seine, hidden below the surface.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:05.570
When the Viking fleet gets close, the Franks
pull the chain up and cause a mass pile-up,

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:08.390
sinking many ships and repelling the invading
force.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:13.100
Perhaps inspired by that very moment, Kattegat
now uses a similar device in its harbor, making

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:16.070
doubly sure that unwelcome ships cannot dock.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:20.030
The chain device that foiled Ragnar and company
when they returned to Frankia was the brainchild

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:23.290
of Rollo, the first Duke of Normandy, and
Ragnar's brother.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:26.930
Overlooked and underappreciated, Rollo decides
to switch sides when the Franks come to him

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:31.110
with an offer: defend Paris from further Viking
attacks, and he would be made a rich man.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:35.210
He is given land, which would become known
as Normandy, and offered the hand of Princess

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.890
Gisla, daughter of Emperor Charles II.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:42.090
It's an uneasy union to begin with, but Gisla
comes to trust and even love her Viking husband

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.620
after he saves her beloved city from his own
kind.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:48.720
In "Vikings: Valhalla," King Æthelred of
England is married to a Norman woman, Queen

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:49.720
Emma.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.020
When he dies, the defense of England is left
to Emma, and she's forced to channel her inner

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.020
Viking.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:57.030
As a member of her court remarks in a throwaway
line that you might have missed, who better

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.670
to defend England from the Vikings than the
great-granddaughter of Rollo himself?

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:02.350
"I will deal with that."

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.840
There are many parallels between the two:
As Emma points out, she was a stranger to

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.960
England when she arrived but was able to master
the language and gain the trust of the people.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.069
Rollo would no doubt be proud of his great-granddaughter,
who proves herself to be a real survivor over

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:17.030
the course of the 1st season.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:21.110
Prince Harald Sigurdsson reveals early on
in "Vikings: Valhalla" that he is the great-grandson

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:23.060
of a man known as Harald Finehair.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.550
That name will probably mean nothing to you
if you've joined the party with "Vikings:

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:29.780
Valhalla," but those who watched "Vikings"
will know that Harald Finehair was a Viking

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.670
king who defied the odds to become king of
all Norway.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:36.140
He believed that it was his destiny to rule
over his homeland, and it put him at odds

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.100
with Ragnar and his sons at times.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:42.300
In Prince Harald's case, his ambitions pit
him against his half-brother, King Olaf.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.940
Olaf Haraldsson has promised Prince Harald
that the throne of Norway will go to him upon

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:49.660
his death, but what he doesn't tell him is
that he's had a son in secret.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:53.520
According to King Canute of Denmark, Olaf
plans to have his son take over after he's

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.460
gone, cutting Harald out of the picture.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:59.370
If Harald joins forces with Canute on his
quest to create a great Northern empire, he

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.580
will guarantee him the Norwegian crown.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:05.010
Will Harald eventually achieve his dream,
just like his great-grandfather did?

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:08.770
That will no doubt be addressed in coming
seasons of "Vikings: Valhalla," but those

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:12.760
of you who are up on your Viking history will
know that Harald Sigurdsson became known as

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.849
Harald Hardrada, meaning Harald "hard ruler."

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:19.370
There are big things to come for Leo Suter's
character.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.180
Both Harald Sigurdsson and Olaf Haraldsson
are Christian Vikings, though the latter is

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.270
far more stern in his beliefs, with little
patience for the old Pagan ways.

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:32.209
However, he pales in comparison to Jarl Kåre,
a zealot who wants to wipe away the Old Gods

00:03:32.209 --> 00:03:33.260
completely.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:37.220
He's absolutely ruthless when it comes to
spreading the word of Jesus Christ, brutally

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.700
murdering any Pagans he comes across.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:43.549
When we see his backstory, the reasons for
his warped worldview become crystal clear

00:03:43.549 --> 00:03:48.320
— Kåre was brought up Pagan, and as a child,
he witnessed his brother die as a human sacrifice

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:49.320
to the gods.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.370
Kåre wears gloves most of the time, and when
he returns to Uppsala, we realize that he's

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.800
been covering up his past — quite literally.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.750
He gains entry to the temple by showing the
guard a Y-shaped symbol tattooed on the palm

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:00.750
of his hand.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:04.680
This symbol is known as Algiz, the ancient
Norse rune of protection.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.450
It's been seen many times in the Vikings universe
— it was on one of the rune stones that

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:13.540
Ubbe and Torvi left for the native North Americans
as gifts upon arriving in the so-called Golden

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:14.819
Land.

00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:18.910
When Jarl Kåre returns to Uppsala posing
as a Pagan, he heads straight to the altar

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:23.170
where his brother had his throat cut to appease
the gods and proceeds to carve his Algiz tattoo

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:24.530
out of his palm.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:28.100
It's a brutal scene, but it's nothing compared
to what he has planned for the priests of

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.470
the holy Pagan site.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:33.820
After talking to the Seer once more, the jarl
and his berserkers go on a rampage, slaughtering

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.900
everyone at Uppsala and burning the prized
temple to the ground.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:40.880
Kåre and his men leave a few kids alive,
but not out of mercy — they want them to

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.510
tell the Pagans of Kattegat that they're next.

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:47.550
That's not quite enough to prove his point,
so Kåre takes a page out of Ragnar's playbook

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:49.210
to really scare his enemies.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:53.070
He beheads the priests and attaches their
severed heads to a boat, which he sends floating

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:54.520
toward Kattegat.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:58.650
Ragnar uses this very tactic while trying
to terrify the Mercians over in England, and

00:04:58.650 --> 00:04:59.840
it works very well.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.770
Still, Kåre knows the people of Kattegat
are a tough breed, so for good measure, he

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:07.400
crucifies the head priest, nailing her to
the mast of the ship.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:12.940
When we return to Kattegat in "Vikings: Valhalla,"
it's being ruled by Jarl Haakon, a Pagan Viking

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:14.169
of African descent.

00:05:14.169 --> 00:05:17.830
She reveals to Freydís Eiríksdóttir that
she's had dreams in which the water next to

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:21.020
Kattegat recedes into the ocean, revealing
a bounty of fish.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:24.840
The people believe it to be a blessing from
the gods, but when the water returns, it causes

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.760
widespread death and destruction.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:30.420
Of course, it's the tsunami that Haakon has
dreamed of, and one that was foreshadowed

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.790
long ago in Season 4 of "Vikings."

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:36.740
The opening credits sequence of "Vikings"
always used the song "If I Had a Heart" by

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:41.200
the Swedish singer-songwriter Fever Ray, though
the imagery was tweaked and updated from season

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.200
to season.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:46.450
In Season 4's opening credits, a brief shot
shows a tsunami wiping out Kattegat, but it

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:49.290
doesn't come from the sea — it comes from
the direction of the valley.

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.650
How can a tsunami come from inland?

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:56.180
As "Vikings: Valhalla" Season 1 nears its
end, Kåre and Olaf launch their assault on

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:57.180
Kattegat.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.199
They trick Haakon and her allies into thinking
they will use the high tide provided by the

00:06:00.199 --> 00:06:04.320
wolf moon to attack from the sea, when in
fact, they plan to come from the other side,

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.270
throwing a wave of Christian warriors at the
city.

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:11.520
While planning his attack, Olaf remarks,
"The walls of Kattegat have not been breached

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.820
in over 100 years."

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:18.020
It's a throwaway comment about a bygone era
to many of his men, but those who watched

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.400
"Vikings" will know exactly what he's talking
about.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:24.040
The Kattegat Civil War was one of the major
events in the first series, fought between

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.940
the sons of Ragnar.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:30.150
Lagertha decides to pounce when they are away
from Kattegat, overthrowing Queen Aslaug.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:34.860
Being Lagertha's son, Bjorn takes her side,
while Aslaug's sons want revenge.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.300
Forces led by Ivar the Boneless assault Kattegat
but are defeated.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:42.430
After calling in a favor from his uncle Rollo
— now a powerful man in Frankia — Ivar's

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:47.040
forces return, and with the help of a thousand
Frankish soldiers, the invading army forces

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.330
Lagertha to flee.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:52.570
Ivar takes power, but he seals his own downfall
by proclaiming himself a god and turning those

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:54.199
closest to him against him.

00:06:54.199 --> 00:06:58.820
The walls of Kattegat are only fully breached
by Bjorn and company because Ivar's wife Freydís

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:00.729
secretly opens a gate and lets them in.

00:07:00.729 --> 00:07:05.290
A century later, Olaf manages to do it by
force — but he has to flee within minutes

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:06.570
of his great victory.

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:10.400
Having secured his son's fleet, Canute's father,
Sweyn Forkbeard, arrives to deal with the

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:11.400
men he deems traitors.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:17.340
What is left of Olaf's army flees at the sight
of his banners, and he's not far behind them.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:21.190
When King Canute of Denmark decides to stay
in England and rule the country, it surprises

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:22.860
Vikings and Saxons alike.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:26.510
He needs the wealth of England to make his
great Northern empire a reality, which is

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:30.889
why he decides to keep Edmund — the recently
crowned replacement of the late King Æthelred

00:07:30.889 --> 00:07:33.520
— as a puppet to control the problematic
English nobles.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:37.440
But before he gets to politics, he needs to
know that all 11 nobles are serious about

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:40.130
their commitment to him, so he has 11 gallows
erected.

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:43.720
He then sends Earl Godwin to fetch the nobles,
who have been locked away.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:47.910
The defiant noble from Northumberland can
be seen fiddling with his fancy rings as he

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:52.400
insults Godwin which is a big mistake: Godwin
invites him to go up and see the new king

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:53.530
first.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:58.000
When the other nobles follow, one of the gallows
is used to kill their counterpart from Northumberland.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:01.980
Canute proceeds to give the remaining nobles
a choice, and as he addresses them, he can

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.610
be seen holding something shiny in his hands.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:08.080
It's clearly one of the rings that the noble
from Northumberland wore so proudly.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.550
He may be the new king of England, but he's
still a Viking at heart.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:16.590
Leif Erikson is one of the most famous Vikings
of all time, credited with establishing a

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:21.069
settlement in North America some 500 years
before Christopher Columbus arrived in the

00:08:21.069 --> 00:08:22.069
New World.

00:08:22.069 --> 00:08:25.389
At the beginning of "Vikings: Valhalla," however,
Leif still lives in the considerable shadow

00:08:25.389 --> 00:08:26.490
of his father.

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:30.430
Erik the Red is a notorious murderer who was
banished from Norway and then from Iceland

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:32.990
for his crimes, forcing him to make a home
on Greenland.

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:38.070
When Leif arrives at Kattegat with his sister,
Freydís Eiríksdóttir, and a crew of Greenlanders,

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:40.750
he's recognized as the son of a dishonorable
man.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:44.560
He's even attacked for it, but he proves that
he's not a killer by sparing the men who tried

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.711
to take his life.

00:08:47.711 --> 00:08:48.740
"You heathen bastard, you're just like your
father."

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:49.760
"You're wrong.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:55.410
If I was just like my father, you'd all be
dead."

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:59.279
Leif later reveals that the same "darkness"
that drove his father lives in him, too, and

00:08:59.279 --> 00:09:03.160
that he only feels calm and content when he's
with fellow Greenlander, Liv.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:07.410
The pair form a romantic relationship as part
of King Canute's army, becoming closer still

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:10.050
after their comrades from the north fall in
battle.

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:13.620
When Liv is killed, the darkness that Leif
spoke of comes to the surface.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:17.550
The Greenlander becomes his infamous father,
and he kills so many men in his rage that

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:21.760
he's literally red, his chest and torso covered
in blood.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:25.240
When the Viking army arrives in England to
avenge the slaughter of their people in the

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:28.790
St. Brice's Day massacre, they have to figure
out how best to attack London.

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:32.740
They decide to follow the advice of King Olaf
and attempt to approach London through the

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:35.240
marshlands, thought impassable by many.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:38.560
Their approach is anticipated, however, and
the scouting party is ambushed.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:41.370
Some of Leif's friends are killed, and he's
presumed dead.

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:44.860
As he appears to be slipping away, he opens
his eyes to see a little local girl looking

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:45.860
down at him.

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:47.570
She places a cross in his hand.

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:51.360
Leif survives, and he can't help but wonder
if the cross had something to do with it.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:55.120
When Liv takes an arrow in the shoulder, and
her chances of pulling through seem slim,

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:58.010
he secretly leaves the cross with her — and
she also survives.

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:01.810
There are several little moments that hint
at Leif moving away from Pagan beliefs and

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:04.880
embracing Christianity, some more subtle than
others.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:09.380
When Jarl Haakon sanctions a human sacrifice
as they prepare to defend Kattegat, others

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:13.410
watch on in awe as the volunteer is killed,
while the look on Leif's face suggests that

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:15.360
he's uncomfortable at best.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.433
Ragnar flirted with Christianity during his
lifetime, and it appears as though Leif will

00:10:18.433 --> 00:10:20.260
do the same.

